 fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Texas remains a Republican-leaning state at the presidential level, with a partisan lean of about six points toward the GOP. The 2028 race is fully open because the incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party starts with an incumbency advantage. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices are available yet, and neither side has reported fundraising.\n\nAttention so far centers on the 2028 primaries, where early surveys show a contested Democratic field and clearer Republican leaders, but those contests say little about how a Texas general election would run. The race therefore leans Republican by roughly six points, with wide uncertainty given the distance to Election Day and the thin statewide evidence. A strong Democratic nominee or a favorable national environment could narrow the margin, while a unified Republican ticket could push Texas toward the safe column.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2466, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2898, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2886, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2879, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2903, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2829, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2913, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2909, "margin": -6.0}]}